{"w":2400,"h":3395,"lines":[{"c":1,"w":0.057995325724283842,"t":"Test 2","y":0.098404340432804216,"x":0.12532332468856169,"h":0.013947133040680848},{"x":0.1255143970676923,"y":0.14244186032923478,"w":0.29012346903483072,"c":1,"t":"READING PASSAGE 2","h":0.01453488398216074},{"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 14-26, which are based on Reading","y":0.17338342662432171,"w":0.77596359252929681,"c":1,"x":0.12539980113373114,"h":0.02097559368371682},{"x":0.12551440230431984,"y":0.19464075410249271,"t":"Passage 2 below.","w":0.15843621571858721,"c":1,"h":0.016195524146054185},{"c":1,"t":"C","x":0.12757201671123738,"h":0.013081395583944611,"y":0.66133720905395799,"w":0.018518517812093099},{"w":0.65879857381184892,"c":1,"x":0.1871045926238773,"y":0.22606860374671134,"t":"Should we try to bring extinct species back to life?","h":0.024262198008974223},{"t":"The passenger pigeon was a legendary species. Flying in vast numbers across","h":0.020021407146476378,"c":1,"y":0.25560014788957375,"x":0.16656306482789981,"w":0.70393452962239578},{"c":1,"t":"North America, with potentially many millions within a single flock, their migration","y":0.27159673084827474,"w":0.71429539998372404,"x":0.16858282704950037,"h":0.02251620343211691},{"w":0.74279836018880219,"h":0.017667844522968212,"t":"was once one of nature's great spectacles. Sadly, the passenger pigeon's existence","c":1,"x":0.16872427662641779,"y":0.28916372228349441},{"t":"came to an end on 1 September 1914, when the last living specimen died at","y":0.30622066360296418,"c":1,"w":0.67716934204101564,"x":0.16862635808388546,"h":0.019278940800643185},{"h":0.019244714114635131,"y":0.32159272230389413,"c":1,"x":0.17064041260267704,"t":"Cincinnati Zoo. Geneticist Ben Novak is lead researcher on an ambitious project","w":0.71219777425130204},{"w":0.68752146402994785,"c":1,"y":0.3384164669098273,"h":0.020542677215468319,"x":0.16655300209293786,"t":"which now aims to bring the bird back to life through a process known as 'de-"},{"w":0.73689107259114572,"h":0.021330012309115909,"x":0.16860868640829202,"t":"extinction'. The basic premise involves using cloning technology to turn the DNA of","y":0.35459514103595524,"c":1},{"h":0.021736147266113837,"c":1,"t":"extinct animals into a fertilised embryo, which is carried by the nearest relative still","x":0.1706046152571552,"y":0.37162902300935929,"w":0.72671819051106767},{"y":0.3884718973807334,"h":0.021518982481759985,"t":"in existence - in this case, the abundant band-tailed pigeon - before being born as","w":0.73489547729492166,"c":1,"x":0.16858280276106191},{"t":"a living, breathing animal. Passenger pigeons are one of the pioneering species in","h":0.021191735149693525,"x":0.17061789257098514,"y":0.40539383675452689,"w":0.72871205647786463,"c":1},{"x":0.16854883343322219,"y":0.42101372770199696,"w":0.73286188761393212,"h":0.022853491865142295,"c":1,"t":"this field, but they are far from the only ones on which this cutting-edge technology"},{"y":0.44316843319065113,"h":0.016195524146054296,"x":0.16872428074657145,"c":1,"t":"is being trialled.","w":0.14609053293863936},{"w":0.68343449910481779,"t":"In Australia, the thylacine, more commonly known as the Tasmanian tiger, is","x":0.16036366077060488,"c":1,"y":0.46442103030757798,"h":0.021924536697154107},{"t":"another extinct creature which genetic scientists are striving to bring back to life.","w":0.71008890787760426,"x":0.17065845858165535,"h":0.019442375192091799,"c":1,"y":0.48185998536255048},{"h":0.019722070233420208,"x":0.1706919210289983,"c":1,"y":0.49805476198125365,"w":0.71010228474934878,"t":"'There is no carnivore now in Tasmania that fills the niche which thylacines once"},{"h":0.019859763281364962,"t":"occupied,' explains Michael Archer of the University of New South Wales. He points","w":0.73887613932291663,"x":0.17068885008639578,"c":1,"y":0.51531190889301659},{"x":0.17061187241113268,"w":0.73698033650716166,"t":"out that in the decades since the thylacine went extinct, there has been a spread in","y":0.53018190811641552,"h":0.02192060293101028,"c":1},{"h":0.019302080601488858,"w":0.72029444376627594,"t":"a 'dangerously debilitating' facial tumour syndrome which threatens the existence","c":1,"x":0.17071734450994094,"y":0.54930436643533587},{"y":0.56504059533043394,"h":0.020662375136736078,"c":1,"w":0.70400131225585949,"x":0.17064896944524161,"t":"of the Tasmanian devils, the island's other notorious resident. Thylacines would"},{"y":0.58193224664215204,"w":0.70602605183919254,"c":1,"h":0.020348323107608257,"t":"have prevented this spread because they would have killed significant numbers","x":0.17065538853015377},{"h":0.021742554519707191,"t":"of Tasmanian devils. 'If that contagious cancer had popped up previously, it would","x":0.17264886732397863,"y":0.59787325990320173,"c":1,"w":0.72263773600260417},{"t":"have burned out in whatever region it started. The return of thylacines to Tasmania","x":0.17267076461126749,"y":0.61469013284257423,"w":0.73077494303385415,"c":1,"h":0.020918683253132175},{"h":0.024304520255404416,"t":"could help to ensure that devils are never again subjected to risks of this kind.'","c":1,"x":0.17260016041260343,"y":0.62925954322614974,"w":0.69605428059895846},{"h":0.022473136443552644,"y":0.65723353057547185,"t":"If extinct species can be brought back to life, can humanity begin to correct the","x":0.16862127615190692,"w":0.70191802978515627,"c":1},{"w":0.71436070760091142,"y":0.67380984651237263,"c":1,"x":0.17266723324965635,"t":"damage it has caused to the natural world over the past few millennia? 'The idea","h":0.021673037390826821},{"y":0.69233881004308651,"c":1,"t":"of de-extinction is that we can reverse this process, bringing species that no","h":0.019045141195380366,"x":0.17272587178065862,"w":0.67307693481445319},{"w":0.73294921875000008,"h":0.023617161457614388,"c":1,"x":0.17058583393116114,"y":0.70522526570864341,"t":"longer exist back to life,' says Beth Shapiro of University of California Santa Cruz's"},{"w":0.68132634480794274,"h":0.019502817250533711,"t":"Genomics Institute.iI don't think that we can do this. There is no way to bring","c":1,"y":0.72503674188253708,"x":0.17476164763948854},{"c":1,"y":0.7414541609616182,"h":0.019703603324395913,"x":0.17476940071133071,"t":"back something that is 100 per cent identical to a species that went extinct a long","w":0.7183593241373698},{"y":0.75731320505519006,"w":0.72659286499023434,"t":"time ago.' A more practical approach for long-extinct species is to take the DNA of","h":0.02060455708677833,"c":1,"x":0.17272627617902078},{"t":"existing species as a template, ready for the insertion of strands of extinct animal","y":0.77464441841484954,"h":0.020685997952841984,"w":0.71847442626953129,"x":0.17473308221241174,"c":1},{"c":1,"t":"DNA to create something new; a hybrid, based on the living species, but which","x":0.17478153164262628,"w":0.69573262532552071,"y":0.79171676325081042,"h":0.019655382675332733},{"t":"looks and\/or acts like the animal which died out.","y":0.81048930630694249,"h":0.015709227751786958,"c":1,"w":0.42816619873046868,"x":0.17272730589801086},{"h":0.013081395583944722,"t":"40","x":0.12962962915391335,"c":1,"w":0.026748971939086902,"y":0.91715116261368301}]}